WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:05.000
We are now ready to answer the question,
are there fragile regions in

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.570
the human genome?
Here is a theorem, yes!

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.640
And here is the proof.
If the Random Breakage Model is correct,

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:20.010
then N rearrangements applied to circular
chromosomes will produce

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:26.335
approximately 2N synteny blocks.
And therefore since there are 280

00:00:26.335 --> 00:00:32.986
human-mouse synteny blocks, there must
have been approximately 280 over 2

00:00:32.986 --> 00:00:39.810
which is 140 2-breaks on the human mouse
evolutionary path.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:45.640
But we have just learned from the 2-Break
Distance Theorem

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:50.270
that there are at least 245
2-breaks on this path.

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:52.240
My next question

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.011
is 245 equal to 140?

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:04.260
It turns out that 245 is much larger than
140, even

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:09.480
if you apply rigorous statistic allowing
for some differences between these

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:15.010
numbers, which means that we arrive at the
contradiction, implying

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:20.500
that one of our assumptions, in the course
of proving this theorem, is

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.690
incorrect.
But which one?

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:27.790
Well, there is not much choice, because we
have made only one assumption.

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:28.630
Here it is.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:35.430
If the Random Breakage Model is correct,
and since we based on this assumption

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:40.580
we arrived to a contradiction, it means
that our assumption was incorrect.

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:44.440
Which implies that Random Breakage Model
failed.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.350
Well, we demonstrated

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:50.600
that Random Breakage Model is incorrect
but of course we have not provided

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:54.710
you with the location of fragile regions
in the human genome and it

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:59.980
is an example of a non constructive proof
when we prove that something is wrong

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:06.190
but cannot provide a certificate showing
where the fragile regions are located.

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:08.480
But if the random breakage model isn't

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:11.570
correct, how would you explain the
remarkable

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:18.030
fit between the short length of synteny
blocks and the exponential distribution?

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:23.010
Well, why have biologists embraced random
breakage model?

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:25.900
Actually it was a logical fallacy.

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:31.050
RBM, Random Breakage Model, is not the
only model that complies with the

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:37.740
exponential distribution test, so it is
correct to claim the Random Breakage Model

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:43.030
implies exponential distribution.
But it is absolutely incorrect to

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:47.970
claim that exponential distribution
implies the Random Breakage Model.

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:52.410
So why was Random Breakage Model refuted?

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:58.040
Because it doesn't comply with the
observed breakpoint reuse.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:03.550
And the question is, is there a model that
complies with both the

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:07.240
exponential distribution and the
breakpoint reuse phenomena?

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:10.956
And it turned out that so called

00:03:10.956 --> 00:03:16.680
Fragile Breakage Model indeed explained
both phenomena.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:18.940
According to the fragile breakage model,

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:23.540
our genomes represent a mosaic or fragile
regions

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:28.988
with this high propensity for arrangements
and solid region that hardly ever broken

00:03:28.988 --> 00:03:30.340
by their arrangements.

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:33.060
And fragile regions which means regions
between

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:37.520
consecutive synteny blocks are short,
accounting, let's

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:41.570
say in the case of human genome for less
than 5% of our genes.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:49.013
Now, the question of course arises, how
FBM, Far Fragile Breakage Model,

00:03:49.013 --> 00:03:54.090
explained both exponential distribution
and the rearrangement hotspots.

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:59.720
Well, a small number of short fragile
regions explain rearrangement hotspots

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:04.760
because there are very few regions where
rearrangements actually can happen.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:09.720
Therefore, they are bound to be repeated
in the same places.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:14.620
Also, if fragile regions are somewhat
randomly distributed throughout

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:19.230
the genome, then the synteny blocks lands
follow exactly

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:24.330
the same exponential distribution, as in
the case of the Random Breakage Model.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.505
And therefore, the Fragile Breakage Model

00:04:26.505 --> 00:04:30.560
explained both exponential distribution
and breakpoint reuse.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:37.086
And should be used as a substitute for the
random breakage model.

00:04:37.086 --> 00:04:41.870
But, maybe fragile breakage model uses the

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:44.490
same logical fallacy that the random
breakage model

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:48.400
uses, because maybe, there is a test that

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.840
was random breakage model and fragile
breakage model fail.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:58.480
It turn out very recently that indeed
there is such a test.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:04.140
And this test became possible because many
genomes have been

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:09.780
sequenced in recent years, and scientists
were able to analyze breakpoints not

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:12.430
only between two genomes, human and mouse,

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:15.900
the first sequenced genome, but between
multiple genomes.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:19.030
And they proposed so-called Multiply
Breakpoint Reuse test

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:24.090
which analyzes breakpoints across
multiply genomes.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:29.290
Unfortunately, we don't have time to go in
the details of the

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:35.090
Multiply Break Point reuse test, but I can
only tell you what follows

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:38.410
from a study that analyzes that.

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:44.250
This recent study revealed evidence for
births and deaths of the fragile

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:49.210
regions, which means that, fragile
regions exist but they're not static.

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:54.540
They move to different locations in human
and mouse genome and the positions of the

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:57.450
fragile regions in the human genome
are different

00:05:57.450 --> 00:06:00.270
than the positions of the fragile regions

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:01.960
in mouse genome.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:06.050
And this discovery resulted in the
Turnover Fragile Breakage Model

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:10.380
which allows for changes, in the location
of fragile regions.

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:14.715
And this new
Turnover Fragile Breakage Model

00:06:14.715 --> 00:06:20.290
actually complies with a new Multiply
Breakpoint Reuse Test.

00:06:20.290 --> 00:06:25.530
Moreover, it actually points to the
location of the currently fragile regions.

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:29.410
They're questions that we were not able to
answer when

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:32.199
we were proving the last theorem from the
last section.

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:39.890
So Turnover Fragile Breakage Model
actually complies with all three tests.

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:45.269
Does it mean that's it's absolutely final
model for chromosome evolution?

00:06:45.269 --> 00:06:47.680
Of course not, there's always
possibilities that

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:50.990
future studies will reveal new and new
tests.

00:06:50.990 --> 00:07:00.395
And will lead to further development of
the of our views of chromosome evolution.

00:07:00.395 --> 00:07:05.850
And with Turnover Fragile Breakage Model
developed,

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:08.980
there are still questions that remain to be
answered.

00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:11.310
Where are the fragile regions located?

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:16.060
And what causes fragility?
And availability of multiplied genomes

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:20.200
will help us eventually to answer this
question and to figure out

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:26.480
what are the currently active fragile
regions in the human genome?

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:31.210
What will be maybe the next reversal that
happened in the human genome.

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:36.220
Let's say the next million years.
And now, after we cover a

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:41.390
different algorithmic aspect of genome
rearrangement, the time has come to

00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:47.520
answer one of the first questions that
appeared in this lesson.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:53.420
How to generate synteny blocks
starting from long genomes?

